TY  - JOUR
TI  - Relational collaboration of multimodal triplet prompts for open-ended object detection
DO  - 10.1016/j.knosys.2026.116326
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105040579252?origin=resultslist
AB  - Open-set object detection has attracted widespread attention in engineering applications owing to its inherent complexity and uncertainty. Different from Open Vocabulary Object Detection (OVOD), which typically requires predefined categories during inference, Open-Ended Object Detection (OEOD) generates category names in a free-form manner. However, significant inconsistencies are observed in both the class name set and its alignment with scene contexts. To address the issue, a relational collaboration framework of multimodal triplet prompts (M2TP) for OEOD is proposed, which aims to capture the semantic relationship between the object category and their corresponding scenarios through the relational collaboration of multimodal prompts. Specifically, a scenario prompt is generated prior to model training by processing input images through Vision-Language Models (VLMs) to create comprehensive scene words description via hierarchical vocabulary matching and synonym expansion mechanisms. The prompt is then forwarded to the Multimodal Triplet Prompts Collaboration (MTPC) module, together with dual-granularity vision prompts at both image and object levels. The MTPC leverages attention mechanism to enhance object features through deep fusion of visual and textual information, enabling better alignment between local object details and global scene context. Lastly, LoRa-enhanced generative large language models are employed to efficiently generate precise object names with improved semantic consistency. Extensive experiments on datasets such as LVIS MiniVal demonstrate that M2TP achieves superior performance compared to the baseline, with notable improvements (+3.9% in APr, +2.2% in AP), effectively addressing the vocabulary generation inconsistency issues in open-world scenarios. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
