TY  - JOUR
TI  - OptiSMR: A unified framework for structured reasoning and self-repairing multi-agent system optimized for generalization
DO  - 10.1016/j.knosys.2026.116143
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105039442221?origin=resultslist
AB  - Optimization problems are ubiquitous across diverse domains; however, their formulation and solution have long relied heavily on expert knowledge. Although large language models show considerable potential for automating such tasks, their limited ability to handle heterogeneous problems, maintain reasoning robustness, and enable autonomous evolution constrains the realization of genuine optimization generalization. To address these challenges, this study proposes OptiSMR, a unified framework designed to advance optimization generalization. Given natural language problem descriptions, OptiSMR employs a prompt-supervised structured chain-of-thought to decompose problems and extract key components such as parameters, variables, objectives, and constraints. This process constructs a universal structured model capable of representing diverse classes of optimization problems. Building upon this foundation, OptiSMR incorporates a multi-agent collaborative architecture that enhances both accuracy and robustness through task specialization and closed-loop error handling. Furthermore, a graph-localized minimum hitting set repair method based on variable-constraint bipartite graphs reformulates error localization and correction as an interpretable optimization process, enabling precise and efficient automatic repair. Moreover, a semantic-guided self-verification mechanism is integrated to suppress the generation of false feasible solutions and to facilitate continuous experience accumulation and autonomous system evolution. Extensive experiments conducted on 6 real-world datasets covering 16 application domains, including resource scheduling, path planning, and manufacturing, demonstrate that OptiSMR can effectively model linear, nonlinear, and mixed-integer programming problems. The proposed framework achieves an average improvement of 8.3% in solution accuracy over an optimal baseline method. © 2026
