TY  - CONF
TI  - Hierarchical Syntax-Aware Modeling for Code Generation and Program Repair
DO  - 10.1109/ICAIRC68035.2025.11385172
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034717537?origin=resultslist
AB  - Large language models used for code generation and repair still face problems with structure and consistency. This paper presents HERALD-Qwen, a hierarchical framework that improves syntax reliability and cross-file coherence. The model includes a multi-level encoder that represents tokens, statements, and functions, a graph neural network that captures syntax tree relations, and a syntax-aware cross-attention mechanism that applies grammar constraints during decoding. A memory-augmented transformer with persistent memory helps maintain long-range context, and reinforcement learning guided by compiler feedback improves the final output. Differential code embedding with contrastive learning helps the model tell correct and incorrect patterns apart, while multi-stage prompting reduces syntax mistakes. Together, these parts make HERALDQwen better at producing syntactically correct and contextually consistent code. Beyond architecture, we add empirical evidence on data augmentation efficacy, explicit metric definitions, and training resource comparisons to address evaluation completeness and reproducibility. © 2025 IEEE.
