TY  - JOUR
TI  - DroneTest-Copilot: LLM-Powered Automated Detection and Repair of Flaws in Drone Autotest Suites
DO  - 10.1109/TNSE.2026.3653678
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105028166683?origin=resultslist
AB  - In the landscape of the low-altitude economy, ensuring the reliability of drone flight control software is of utmost importance. ArduPilot's autotest is an automated testing framework integrated into its continuous integration (CI) pipeline, which is crucial for validating autopilot functionality. However, numerous logic defects exist in its test cases, where the intended workflow doesn't match the code implementation. This paper presents DroneTest-Copilot (DTC), a novel framework that leverages large language models (LLMs) to automate the validation and correction of utotest test cases. The system consists of four components: Preprocessor, Test Case Processor, Flaw Corrector, and Vulnerability Tester. It extracts relevant context from pull requests, uses LLMs to detect flaws such as parameter mismatch, API misuse, execution order errors, and logic errors, and then generates corrected test code. We also conduct a detailed survey and comparative analysis of existing related works to highlight the novelty and positioning of DTC. Evaluations on a dataset of patched and new test case pull requests show that DTC can correctly identify 87.23% of introduced defects in the verification set and discover previously unknown vulnerabilities in the new test case set. DTC substantially reduces manual effort in maintaining autotest suites and uncovers subtle flaws in test case code, enhancing the reliability of drone flight control software. © 2013 IEEE.
