TY  - CONF
TI  - An Autonomous Agentic Framework for Interactive Code Mentoring and Error Resolution using Large Language Models
DO  - 10.1109/MEDCOM67532.2025.11404910
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036640568?origin=resultslist
AB  - Revolutionary advances in large language models have ushered in the age of intelligent agents. The development of artificial general intelligence may hinge on Large Language Model (LLM) agents, which are capable of goal-driven behavior and dynamic adaptability. The purpose of this study is to bridge this gap with an autonomous Agentic framework for interactive code mentoring and bug fixing powered by LLMs, specifically GPT-4o-mini and built on the MERN stack. The software platform enables authenticated users to pose code-specific issues in a structured, prompt-response manner and receive an intelligent response, while logging all their interactions, including prompt content, response time, and token utilization. During testing, the system processed over 150 user prompts, with 71% of responses categorized as slow (above 1000 ms), 29% moderate (500-1000 ms), and none classified as fast (below 500 ms). Token analysis showed an average usage of 480 total tokens per prompt, with notable spikes at prompts #3 and #5. The platform comes equipped with a simple user interface, history management of sorts, and an admin tool to visualize data captured at the prompt level. The Agentic framework and development may not only assist individual learning but may produce organized information to analyze their beliefs and morals.  © 2025 IEEE.
