TY  - CONF
TI  - Logs In, Patches Out: Automated Vulnerability Repair via Tree-of-Thought LLM Analysis
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105021307111?origin=resultslist
AB  - Research on automated vulnerability repair often requires extensive program analysis and expert input, making it challenging to deploy in practice. We propose SAN2PATCH, a system that generates patches using only sanitizer logs and source code, eliminating the need for costly program analysis or manual intervention. SAN2PATCH employs multi-stage reasoning with Large Language Models (LLMs) to decompose the patching process into four distinct tasks: vulnerability comprehension, fault localization, fix strategy formulation, and patch generation. Through tree-structured prompting and rigorous validation, SAN2PATCH can generate diverse, functionally-correct patches. Evaluations on the VulnLoc dataset show that SAN2PATCH successfully patches 79.5% of vulnerabilities, surpassing state-of-the-art tools like ExtractFix (43%) and VulnFix (51%) by significant margins. On our newly curated SAN2VULN dataset of 27 new vulnerabilities from various open-source projects, SAN2PATCH achieves a 63% success rate, demonstrating its effectiveness on modern security flaws. Notably, SAN2PATCH excels at patching complex memory-related vulnerabilities, successfully fixing 81.8% of buffer overflows while preserving program functionality. This high performance, combined with minimal deployment requirements and elimination of manual steps, makes SAN2PATCH a practical solution for real-world vulnerability remediation. © 2025 by The USENIX Association All Rights Reserved.
