TI  - An Empirical Analysis of ESLint Information Components and Their Interactions in LLM-based Program Repair
DO  - 10.1109/APSEC66846.2025.00084
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105035190248?origin=resultslist
AB  - Static analysis tools like ESLint are widely used for code quality assurance, yet optimizing their integration with LLM-based Automated Program Repair remains underexplored. While existing approaches typically use complete static analysis reports, the impact of individual information components and their interactions has received limited attention. This paper presents an empirical analysis of 12 ESLint information components' impact on GPT-4 Turbo's repair performance. Through experiments on the complete TFix test dataset (10,504 cases), we find: (1) Concise components (e.g., linter messages) show high performance per token, while verbose components show diminishing returns in combination. (2) Components exhibit interaction effects; some components with low individual performance show high importance in combinations. (3) Strategic component selection based on interaction analysis can achieve comparable performance to using all components while reducing token usage. Our findings suggest that prompt optimization for LLM-based APR can benefit from analyzing both individual component contributions and their interactions. © 2025 IEEE.
