TY  - JOUR
TI  - A Hybrid Convolutional and Enhanced Vision Transformer Model for Improving Turmeric Plant Disease Detection
DO  - 10.22266/ijies2026.0331.04
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105030381123?origin=resultslist
AB  - Early plant diseases prevention in turmeric crop can enhance yield, improve food production and drive economic gains. Among other existing models, Convolutional and Vision Transformer model for Turmeric Diseases Detection (ConViT-TDD) has demonstrated superior performance in predicting turmeric plant diseases. It integrates a self-attention mechanism, Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and the YOLOV3-Tiny model to enhance the models performances. ViT used to enhance YOLOV3 demands significant data and memory, requiring modifications to fit limited compute resources. Additionally, ViT is complex and lacks locality inductive bias due to non-overlapping patches, limiting cross-patch attention. This results in overly smooth attention distributions, reducing focus on important tokens. To solve this, Location Shifted Patch Generation (LSPG), Rotated Position Embedding (RPE), Local Context Attention (LCA) and Combative Loss Function (CLF) are proposed in this paper to enhance ViT performance in ConViT-TDD. In this model, ViT with a single transformer encoder block is introduced to model the images as patch sequences for capturing long-range dependencies while eliminating deep stacked encoders. By fusing Multi-Head Self-Attention (MHSA) and a Feed-Forward Network (FFN) into single layer, the proposed model reduces computational complexity, accelerates experimentation and enables deployment on resource-constrained devices. When combined with CNN features, the proposed model supports accurate predictions. To address the limitations of non-overlapping patches in ViT, LSPG is employed for patch embedding by rotating, concatenating and splitting those images into overlapping patches for effective tokenization. RPE further refines positional embeddings by encoding relative distances between tokens derived from LSPG. LCA enhances local information by integrating spatial contextual cues through Local Coordinate Convolution (LCC) and feature refinement to capture intricate spatial dependencies. Finally, YOLOV3-Tiny is applied for turmeric plant diseases prediction. In addition, Cross-Entropy (CE) loss is utilized to improve training stability. The proposed model is termed as Convolutional and Enhanced ViT Model for Turmeric Diseases detection (ConEViT-TDD). The evaluation result indicate that the ConEViT-TDD model attains a total accuracy, precision, recall and F1-score values of 94%, 93.87%, 93.93% and 93.82%, respectively on the Mendeley turmeric plant diseases dataset outperforming YOLOv3-Tiny, YOLOv3, EfficientNetV2 and ViT-YOLOv3 baseline models. © This article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License. License details: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/
